anubis (1.27.0-0+keel2) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Serve the challenge to clients that do not accept gzip
    (debian/patches/challenge-without-gzip.patch). Upstream refused one
    such request in 64 at random with HTTP 500, and Keel Web answered 500
    to those clients (Keel-Linux/keel-web#2). A missing Accept-Encoding
    accepts any coding; the proof of work is the defence. The patch adds
    a test of 4096 requests without gzip, all challenged.
  * Accept IPv6 literals ([2001:db8::1]) in REDIRECT_DOMAINS
    (debian/patches/redirect-domains-ipv6.patch): Keel Web sets it to the
    machine's names and addresses to close pass-challenge's open
    redirect, and Anubis refused to start on an IPv6 entry.
  * debian/rules: TestChallengeFor_ErrNotFound, skipped as flaky because
    of that refusal, runs again.
  * autopkgtest: 512 requests without Accept-Encoding through nginx all
    get the challenge page with HTTP 200.

 -- Marcos Méndez <mendez.foto@gmail.com>  Fri, 02 Oct 2026 04:00:00 +0000

anubis (1.27.0-0+keel1) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Keel Linux build for trixie, based on the Debian Go team's
    work-in-progress packaging (salsa go-team/packages/anubis, ITP
    #1102132), which is kept as the debian/sid branch of this repository.
  * New upstream release 1.27.0, from upstream's signed
    anubis-src-vendor tarball (Go modules vendored) plus an npm component
    with the nine runtime JavaScript dependencies at the lockfile's
    versions; the build never uses the network.
  * Build with golang-1.26-go (trixie-backports), as go.mod requires
    1.26.3; build the challenge assets with trixie's esbuild, and xess
    with esbuild instead of postcss and cssnano.
  * Ship anubis@.service: a disabled template, DynamicUser, hardened; it
    refuses to start without /etc/anubis/<instance>.key, the signing key,
    which it copies into the runtime directory for the dynamic user.
  * Ship /etc/anubis/default.env (loopback only) and the upstream
    default policy as /etc/anubis/botPolicies.yaml.
  * Add an autopkgtest: the instance behind nginx challenges a browser
    and an unverified Googlebot, and refuses to start without a key.

 -- Marcos Méndez <mendez.foto@gmail.com>  Thu, 01 Oct 2026 12:00:00 +0000

